GEO:39.167655;-86.523537
SUMMARY:Such Now the Necessity: The Declaration and Secession Symposium
DESCRIPTION:The symposium considers the Declaration specifically as a sec
 essionist document: how it functioned in the early United States\, how it
  relates to broader theories of secession\, and how it has been or might 
 be used in other political contexts.  The Declaration of Independence is 
 revered as the formative expression of American values. However\, it was 
 designed primarily as a legal and rhetorical address to the world—"to t
 he opinions of mankind"—justifying a radical project: secession. The De
 claration advances the two principal arguments that are familiar justific
 ations used by secession movements today: an inherent right to self-gover
 nment based on "consent of the governed\," and "remedial secession" in re
 sponse to "a long train of abuses." Given this founding DNA\, why is the 
 United States no longer a pro-secession advocate? What would it mean to t
 ake the Declaration's arguments seriously today—both in America's forei
 gn relations and for America's own territories (such as Puerto Rico) and 
 the 50 states?  Schedule:  Panel 1. The Declaration and Secession in the 
 early United States 10:30 to 12:00Kon Dierks\, Indiana University  Eric S
 lauter\, University of Chicago  Noah Eber-Schmidt\, Indiana University  T
 imothy William Waters\, Indiana University (moderator)    Panel 2. The De
 claration\, American Territories\, and Latin America 1:30 to 3:00 p.m.Guy
 -Uriel Charles\, Harvard University  Christina D. Ponsa-Kraus\, Columbia 
 University  Emmanuel Hiram Arnaud\, Boston University  Luis Fuentes Rohwe
 r\, Indiana University (moderator)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The symposium considers the Declaration
  specifically as a secessionist document: how it functioned in the early 
 United States\, how it relates to broader theories of secession\, and how
  it has been or might be used in other political contexts.</p><p>  The De
 claration of Independence is revered as the formative expression of Ameri
 can values. However\, it was designed primarily as a legal and rhetorical
  address to the world—"to the opinions of mankind"—justifying a radic
 al project: secession. The Declaration advances the two principal argumen
 ts that are familiar justifications used by secession movements today: an
  inherent right to self-government based on "consent of the governed\," a
 nd "remedial secession" in response to "a long train of abuses." Given th
 is founding DNA\, why is the United States no longer a pro-secession advo
 cate? What would it mean to take the Declaration's arguments seriously to
 day—both in America's foreign relations and for America's own territori
 es (such as Puerto Rico) and the 50 states?</p><p>  Schedule:</p><p>  Pan
 el 1. The Declaration and Secession in the early United States 10:30 to 1
 2:00</p><ul><li>Kon Dierks\, Indiana University  </li><li>Eric Slauter\, 
 University of Chicago  </li><li>Noah Eber-Schmidt\, Indiana University  <
 /li><li>Timothy William Waters\, Indiana University (moderator)  </li></u
 l><p>  Panel 2. The Declaration\, American Territories\, and Latin Americ
 a 1:30 to 3:00 p.m.</p><ul><li>Guy-Uriel Charles\, Harvard University  </
 li><li>Christina D. Ponsa-Kraus\, Columbia University  </li><li>Emmanuel 
 Hiram Arnaud\, Boston University  </li><li>Luis Fuentes Rohwer\, Indiana 
 University (moderator)  </li></ul>
UID:20261002T140000Z-2428181@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260923T151407Z
